DNSSEC support in Dnsruby
=========================

DNSSEC defines a set of security extensions to DNS which provide a way for a resolver to verify cryptographically the DNS RRSets returned by an upstream resolver. The main standard is defined in RFCs 4033, 4034 and 4035.

Dnsruby provides a recursive, validating security-aware stub resolver which maintains a cache of trusted keys and verifies RRSIG-signed messages with those keys (adding new trusted keys from signed DNSKEY RRSets and DS records). If dnsruby does not currently have the required key, it will attempt to walk the tree from the nearest known trusted key.

The dnssec security status of a message is stored in Message#security_level (defined by Message::SecurityLevel).

It is possible to tell Dnsruby to use a Recursor or a defined (or system default) Resolver to perform the validation. The default is to use a Recursor, as many systems are behind dodgy servers which mangle the DNS records. Using a Recursor means that only authoritative nameservers are queried for the DNSSEC records.

Dnsruby includes the IANA root DS trust anchors for KSK-2017 (key tag 20326) and KSK-2024 (key tag 38696). KSK-2024 began signing the root DNSKEY RRSet on 11 October 2026. The revoked KSK-2010 (key tag 19036) is no longer trusted. Applications may also configure local DNSKEY or DS trust anchors using Dnsruby::Dnssec.add_trust_anchor. Obtain trust anchors through a trusted channel; IANA publishes the root anchors and their validity periods at https://www.iana.org/dnssec/files. Dnsruby does not automatically update these configured anchors using RFC 5011, so they must be kept current through software or application updates.

It is possible to turn off dnssec validation on a per-message basis. Simply set Message#do_validation to false.

Resolver#do_validation defaults to false; set it to true to enable local DNSSEC validation. The dnssec flag controls requesting DNSSEC records and is distinct from local validation. Recursor#query attempts validation unless its no_validation argument is true. Missing root anchors affect queries that need to establish trust from the root; an application using local trust anchors or previously authenticated cached keys may behave differently.

Dnsruby maintains a cache of responses, and a cache of trusted keys. Once the initial keys have been downloaded, and a set of trusted keys built up, very little overhead is required to enjoy the benefits of DNSSEC. There is, however, some initial cost (to build up the caches).

Dnsruby::Dnssec.clear_trusted_keys clears learned DNSKEYs and all DS/DLV anchors, including the built-in root DS anchors; configured DNSKEY anchors are retained. To remove local anchors and built-in root trust, call clear_trust_anchors followed by clear_trusted_keys. Use remove_trust_anchor or clear_trust_anchors to remove local anchors explicitly. Dnsruby::Dnssec.reset restores the built-in root anchors, clears local anchors and cached keys, and resets the validation policy and resolver settings to their defaults.

DNSSEC validation evidence
--------------------------

Every local validation records how it reached its conclusion. After a query
(set Resolver#do_validation and Resolver#dnssec both true), the returned
Message carries a Dnsruby::ValidationEvidence object in
Message#validation_evidence:

    response = res.query('example.com', Types.A)
    evidence = response.validation_evidence
    evidence.to_json              # portable, self-contained document
    evidence.conclusion           # "SECURE", "INSECURE", "BOGUS", ...

The document contains only the material the validator used: the configured or
built-in trust anchor, each delegation's signed DS and the parent key that
authenticated it, the child DNSKEY RRSet and the DS-matched key, the selected
data signature, and the signed NSEC/NSEC3 records behind an authenticated
denial. Records are content addressed by the SHA-256 of their canonical wire
form and are referenced by steps, so the same key is never duplicated.

Candidate signatures that were present but not selected are listed under
"candidates"; they are deliberately excluded from the explanation digest. A
successful path records the one signature and key that were actually checked.

When a response comes from the cache, the cached Message keeps its original
security level and evidence, so a cache hit can still explain itself.

A later validation may reuse a key learned during an earlier one. That export
does not pretend the key was configured: it starts at a `learned_key` step and
embeds the earlier proof that established the key, which replay rechecks too.

On failure the steps that were reached are retained, including the specific
signature or delegation that failed. Failed attempts are labelled separately
from the attempt that produced the conclusion, so an unsuccessful policy
attempt is never drawn as part of a later successful path.

Offline replay

ValidationEvidence#replay rechecks the recorded signatures and trust edges
without any network access. Pass the time and policy the conclusion was
reached at:

    result = evidence.replay(time: evidence.validation_time, policy: evidence.policy)
    result.security_level       # reproduces the recorded conclusion
    result.explanation_digest   # unchanged proof identity

    Dnsruby::ValidationEvidence.replay(File.read('evidence.json'),
      time: Time.utc(2026, 11, 14, 12),
      policy: JSON.parse(File.read('policy.json')))

Replay fails with Dnsruby::ValidationEvidence::ReplayError when a required
record is removed, a signature no longer verifies at the fixed time, a
recorded DS does not match its DNSKEY, a denial proof does not cover the
query, or the policy no longer allows the recorded trust anchor. Adding an
unrelated valid record does not change the explanation digest or the replay
result.

The integrity seal covers the query, time, policy, used steps and the exact
bytes of each referenced record. It does not cover unused candidates or
records, so an independent auditor can add a legitimate extra signature
without changing the explanation, while any change to a used record is
detected.

A command-line entry point is included for auditors without a Ruby harness:

    bin/dnssec-evidence replay evidence.json \
      --time 2026-11-14T12:00:00Z --policy policy.json

It prints the reproduced conclusion, cache provenance and explanation digest,
and exits non-zero when the evidence no longer supports the conclusion.
